ssing or search work.\n5. **Search.** Order classes bytewise by label. If every class is a\n   singleton, assign `1 … n` in that order and stop. Otherwise take the\n   first non-singleton class and, **for each of its members in turn**,\n   give that member the label that is the CBOR byte string of\n   `[:ind <depth> <old label>]`, where `depth` is 0 at the top-level\n   search and increases by one per level of recursion (distinct from\n   every refinement label by its tag; no ordering relative to other\n   labels is needed), refine to stability, and\n   recurse. Every branch is explored. Each complete leaf yields a\n   numbering; build the §5 rows under it and take the **root body's\n   canonical CBOR bytes** (the digest preimage, not the digest) as the\n   leaf's key. The procedure's output is the row set of the leaf with\n   the bytewise least key. Two leaves with equal keys have emitted\n   identical rows: they differ only in which source cell received\n   which number, which the transported representation cannot observe.\n\n**Validation.** Canonicality is a property of the emitted rows, not of\na source-cell-to-number bijection, so the receiver does not compare\nnumberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\nprocedure, re-emits rows, and compares the resulting root id with the\ncarried root id: unequal is `:yin.k/undecodable` kind\n`:noncanonical-cells`. Every labeling that yields the selected\n             (crypt/byteArrayToHex (.digest hasher)))\n     :cljd (.toString (.convert crypto/sha256 bs))))\n\n\n(def default-hash-algorithm\n  \"The default algorithm used for implicit minting (content-hash, segment-key,\n   materialize!). Never consulted when verifying an existing address.\"\n  :blake3)\n\n\n(def registry\n  \"The closed, immutable algorithm registry. Both initial entries produce\n   32-byte (64 lowercase hex character) digests.\"\n      v)))\n\n\n(defn code-address\n  \"`:yin.code/hash` of a canonical vector: its `dao.jing` segment address.\n   UCF mints no second addressing scheme.\"\n  [v]\n  (jing/segment-key v))\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 17:52:51 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 00:52:51 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `94c65df74a9bad25d4486b7d806f5529d87477ff` and its preceding-commit diff. No files edited.\n\n**LOW — clarification, non-blocking | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:122 | “Jing’s default at version 3” must be an immutable version mapping, not the reader’s current default.** The text’s version-bump requirement establishes that intention. | Prefer the explicit sentence: “Body version 3 uses BLAKE3 permanently; later defaults require another carrier version. Existing version-3 verification and re-emission retain BLAKE3.”\n\nA. **Uniqueness passes.** Binding one digest to an immutable carrier revision supplies the required canonical address policy. The one-argument call currently matches A, as the owner requested. Future implementations must preserve historical version mappings.\n\nB. **Numbering is not algorithm-independent.** Root-body bytes contain child-row addresses; changing their digest changes those bytes and can change which leaf wins. Canonical child-task addresses also enter parent class encodings. This is compatible with the proposal: numbering remains deterministic within each carrier version, while another version may choose different numbers. Re-emission must use the input version’s digest, never whichever default is current.\n\nC. **No remaining operative SHA-256 pin found.** The remaining mentions describe self-identifying address syntax and the historical rejected choice. Code addresses retain their governing function.\n\nThe digest change preserves the census commitment, validation discipline, and native-execution boundary. No blocking architectural defect was found. Publication remains publication of a Draft.\n\nVERDICT: READY_TO_MERGE"}}
